                                                     LIBORDER(11)NAME     liborder - Produce library ordering information for OS-9     modules.SYNOPSIS     liborder [keys] file1.r file2.r ...DESCRIPTION     This utility has been designed to build a best possible ord-     ering scheme for OS-9 libraries. Due to how the OS-9 linker     works while scanning libraries, the ordering scheme with     which modules are concatenated to produce a library is     important. In fact, when the linker needs to link the symbol     Sa, it scans the library to extract the module A which con-     tains that symbol. Then it analyzes module A trying to     resolve all the unsatisfied references. To do so it contin-     ues to scan the library. Now suppose that module A needs     symbol Sb contained in module B. If module B appears before     A in the library, the linker cannot find it and the only     solution is to specify twice the library on the command line     for the linker. On the other end, a proper choice of the     module order can solve this problem.     Liborder reads as input OS-9 ROF modules, extract the infor-     mation about PUBLIC and EXTERNAL symbols and prints on stan-     dard output the ordered list of the modules. This list may     be used to build the relative library.     Liborder can also discover cycles in the module list. A     cycle exists when module A calls a routine in module B which     calls another routine in module A again (possibly passing     through other modules). In this case a possible way to     improve the linking speed is to recompile all the modules of     the cycle in a single source file.KEYS     -modinfo  Print PUBLIC and EXTERNAL symbols for each input               module.     -public   Print the list of all the PUBLIC symbols. A symbol               is PUBLIC if defined inside the module itself (it               is exported). I.e., routine names defined in a               module are PUBLIC.     -external Print the list of all the EXTERNAL symbols. A sym-               bol is EXTERNAL is referenced but not defined               inside the module (it is imported). I.e., refer-               ences to library routines are EXTERNAL symbols.     -preced   Print the set of the precedences between modules.               Module A precedes module B if A references a PUB-               LIC symbol of B. The output list if formed by               lines of the type: predecessor "<<<" successor.     -precsymb Like option "preced" but the symbol name which               caused the reference is also printed. This symbol               is usually a routine's name, but it might also be               a variable name (but not in FORTRAN).     -exit     Print the above information and exit. No ordering               is performed and printed.     -lump     Print the set of fundamental cycles. All the               cycles in the input module list are printed. It is               suggested that modules in a cycle are put togheter               in the same source file and recompiled. This will               semplify and speed-up the linking process. If this               option is specified, the ordering of the modules               is not performend and printed.EXAMPLE     The following script file builds the library mylib.l in the     current directory.     #! /bin/sh     echo -n "cat " > liborder.junk     liborder *.r | tr ' 12' " " >> liborder.junk     # change the "mylib.l" to whatever you like     echo "> mylib.l" >> liborder.junk     chmod +x liborder.junk     liborder.junk  # run cat and build the TRUE library     rm liborder.junkCAVEATS     FORTRAN common block are handled correctly.     With very long list of ROF modules, the program execution     might be very slow.SEE ALSO     unpacklib(1), MicrowareAUTHORS     Antonio Pastore, CERN, CN/CE/MS